#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "7f3646c9-89b6-58de-a363-cb87e034d9f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5333" :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/d05aa50677877b505dd8f37cac612630.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,4)$.\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n$y^2 = 4px$.\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,4)$, we can substitute $x = -8$ and $y = 4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(-8) \\\\\n16 &= -32p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\\ny^{2} &= -2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096424 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d66f0111-fe31-5381-ab6d-cd67ee403d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-5334" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(4,4)$." :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n$y^2 = 4px$.\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(4,4)$, we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(4) \\\\\n16 &= 16p \\\\\np &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(1)x \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096425 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "5546750b-01ad-5aaa-93de-b223297ffc0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5335" :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/ba4f53d4bcb8d7c3ce8e264ba354d250.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,6)$.\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - k)^{2} &= 4p(x - h)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (4,2)\n$$\n\nwe substitute $h=4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,6)$, so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4) \\\\\n(6 - 2)^{2} &= 4p(0 - 4) \\\\\n16 &= -16p \\\\\np &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4(-1)(x - 4) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -4(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -1$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096426 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/id #uuid "9a27e39b-4a17-578f-830b-3e143c65fd55" :question/math-academy-id "e-5336" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-6,2)$." :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - k)^{2} &= 4p(x - h)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (2,-2)\n$$\n\nwe substitute $h=2$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-6, 2)$, so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2) \\\\\n(2 + 2)^{2} &= 4p(-6 - 2) \\\\\n16 &= -32p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2) \\\\\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x - 2) \\\\\n(y + 2)^{2} &= -2(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a17f756f-01f7-5ddf-87d1-f03c882c5a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093891} {:answer/id #uuid "adb5274e-2ea8-5709-b15c-c9a9a1c7a4f7" :answer/type {:db/iden